 36AR    COULOMB EXCITATION:XUNDL-8    2017VO05                           201708
 36AR c  Compiled (unevaluated) dataset from 2017Vo05:                          
 36AR2c  Phys Rev C 96, 024305 (2017)                                           
 36AR c  Compiled by B. Singh (McMaster) August 19, 2017                        
 36AR c  2017Vo05: Beam=1.75 MeV/nucleon {+36}Ar from ISAC-II facility of       
 36AR2c  TRIUMF. Target=0.43 mg/cm{+2} thick carbon evaporated onto a           
 36AR3c  22.2 mg/cm{+2} Au backing. Measured E|g, (particle)|g-coin, half-life  
 36AR4c  of the first 2+ state by Doppler-shift attenuation method (DSAM) using 
 36AR5c  TRIUMF-ISAC Gamma-Ray Escape Suppressed Spectrometer (TIGRESS)         
 36AR6c  containing 13 clover Ge detectors                                      
 36AR7c  for |g-ray detection, and 44-element silicon PIN diode array           
 36AR8c  for scattered particle detection. Deduced B(E2)|_ for first 2+ to g.s. 
 36AR9c  E2 transition, and compared with previous experimental results         
 36ARAc  determined in Coulomb excitation and DSAM measurements, and the        
 36ARBc  evaluated data.                                                        
 36AR  L  0.0         0+                                                        
 36AR  L 1970.3       2+                340 FS   35                             
 36AR cL T$measured mean lifetime |t=490 fs {I50} (2017VaAA) from DSAM and      
 36AR2cL Monte Carlo |g-ray line-shape simulations using several approaches.    
 36AR3cL In the first analysis method, experimental |g-ray line shapes were     
 36AR4cL compared to simulated line shapes generated within the Lindhard        
 36AR5cL formalism with multiple nuclear scattering and stopping modeled under  
 36AR6cL a Monte Carlo technique, and the mean lifetimes obtained were:         
 36AR7cL 557 fs {I58} for minimum of reduced |h{+2} approach, and 532 fs {I58}  
 36AR8cL using ratio R=R(stopped)/R(shifted) as a function of simulated         
 36AR9cL lifetime. In the second analysis method, more comprehensive Monte Carlo
 36ARAcL GEANT4 simulations were used, where the two corresponding mean         
 36ARBcL lifetimes were: 489 fs {I51} for minimum of reduced |h{+2} approach,   
 36ARCcL and 484 fs {I50} from ratio R=R(stopped)/R(shifted) as a function of   
 36ARDcL simulated lifetime. The latter method was considered by the authors as 
 36AREcL more robust, thus they adopted the average value of mean lifetime      
 36ARFcL |t=490 fs {I50}, keeping a 2-significant-figure result. This value can 
 36ARGcL be compared with |t=456 fs {I+18-29} in 2016Pr01 (At. Data and Nucl.   
 36ARHcL Data Tables 107, 1) evaluation of model-independent methods.           
